nts an immediate lethal threat from Black. Black has formed a "Live 4" (also known as an Open 4) on the diagonal consisting of stones C10, D9, E8, and F7. This pattern is open at both ends (B11 and G6), meaning Black can play at either spot on their next turn to form a 5-in-a-row and win. White has no immediate winning move (such as a 4-in-a-row that can be extended to 5) to override this threat. Consequently, White\'s only valid option is to block one of these two critical points to prevent an immediate loss.\n\nReason: White must block at G6 or B11. Between the two, G6 is the better move positionally. By playing G6, White not only blocks the diagonal threat but also connects with existing White stones at H7 and I8 to form a "Sleeping 3" (G6-H7-I8). This creates a defensive structure and retains some potential for future attacks. In contrast, playing at B11 would block the threat but only form a weak 2-stone connection on the edge of the board with C11.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 6}'}
